    <h2>1. The Geometry Problem: Why Inside 45-Degree Miters Fail</h2>
    <p>
      In an ideal CAD drafting world, interior wall corners meet at exactly 90.0 degrees. In real residential construction, two sheets of 1/2-inch drywall meet in the corner, covered by three coats of joint compound (drywall mud) and paper tape.
    </p>
    <p>
      Drywall tapers intentionally build up mud in corners to feather the tape smooth. As a result, the bottom 6 inches of an inside corner almost always measures between <strong>92 degrees and 95 degrees</strong> (obtuse). When you cut two baseboards at 45 degrees and press them into a 93-degree corner:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>The <em>toes</em> (front edges) touch, but the <em>heels</em> (backs) stay open against the wall.</li>
      <li>If you push the boards tight against the wall, the front toes separate, leaving a gaping V-shaped wedge gap.</li>
      <li>Even if heavily caulked, winter heating drives humidity down from 50% to 20%. Solid wood and MDF shrink in thickness, shearing the caulk bead and cracking the seam open.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>2. The Physics of the Coped Joint</h2>
    <p>
      A coped joint completely bypasses the out-of-square wall corner problem:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Piece 1 (Square Butt Cut):</strong> The first baseboard runs completely past the corner and butts dead-flat into the drywall corner. It does not matter if the corner is 88 degrees or 95 degrees.</li>
      <li><strong>Piece 2 (Coped Overlap):</strong> The intersecting board is cut to the exact reverse profile of the baseboard face. It laps over the face of Piece 1 like a puzzle piece.</li>
      <li><strong>Self-Sealing Action:</strong> If the wall expands, shifts, or shrinks, Piece 1 simply slides microscopically behind Piece 2. The visible joint line remains 100% tight without ever opening a crack.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>3. The 3-Step Coping Technique</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Cut an Inside 45-Degree Miter on the Miter Saw:</strong> Place the baseboard upright against the fence (or flat if bevel cutting) and cut an inside 45-degree angle. This cut exposes the end grain and highlights the exact edge profile of the molding face.</li>
      <li><strong>Highlight the Profile Line:</strong> Run a carpenter's pencil flat along the crisp painted edge where the cut wood meets the pre-primed face. This creates a bold graphite cutting line.</li>
      <li><strong>Undercut with a Coping Saw (10&deg; to 15&deg; Back-Bevel):</strong> Using a fine-toothed coping saw (or a rotary flap disc on an angle grinder for pros), follow the pencil line precisely. Tilt the saw blade backward at a 10-to-15 degree angle away from the face. This creates a sharp, knife-like front edge with zero back-wood interference, allowing the face edge to bed down effortlessly over the adjoining board.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>4. Comparison: Coping vs Inside Miter vs Corner Blocks</h2>
