          mistake: "Cutting Straight Across without Back-Beveling",
          impact: "Holding the coping saw blade at 90 degrees leaves wood behind the front edge. When you test fit, the hidden back wood hits the adjoining board first, holding the visible face line 1/16-inch open.",
          prevention: "Always tilt the coping saw backward at a 15-degree undercut so only the razor-thin front face touches the flat board."
        },
        {
          mistake: "Coping Both Ends of a Single Board",
          impact: "Trying to cope both ends of one piece makes installation nearly impossible to wedge into place between two existing corner boards.",
